{"answer":"The critic's exponential-path objection conflates explicit path enumeration with the polynomial-cost verification that the pass actually requires. Determining whether every defense path from an IN node terminates in an unattacked IN node does not require materializing all exponentially many paths: it reduces to a standard reachability question on the defense subgraph induced by IN nodes — namely, whether any IN node on a defense walk from the target reaches a cycle of IN nodes without encountering an unattacked IN root. That property is decidable in polynomial time by single-source reachability plus cycle detection (DFS or SCC decomposition) on the IN-induced subgraph. A node is acyclic-defended iff every IN-reachable defense chain bottoms out at an unattacked IN node; equivalently, no IN node reachable along the defense relation lies on a directed cycle within the IN subgraph unless an unattacked root is reachable on every such path. Formulating the condition as universal path termination is a semantic specification, not an algorithmic mandate; the existence of exponentially many paths does not entail exponential verification cost any more than 'all reachable nodes are marked' requires enumerating all walks. Therefore the critic has not established that the second pass is super-polynomial, and the polynomial-cost requirement is not shown to be violated."}